    <div class="panel panel-default">
        <div class="panel-heading">News Stream</div>
            
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>US Weekly Jobless Claims Fall to 1-Year Low</b>
                    <div class="comment more">The number of Americans filing for unemployment benefits dropped to 684 thousand in the week ended March 20th, its lowest since the pandemic hit the labor market in March 2020 and well below market expectations of 730 thousand, adding to signs of a gradual job recovery. Claims are expected to fall in the coming weeks following the approval of President Biden's relief package in early March and as several states ease coronavirus-induced restrictions amid the rapid pace of vaccination. On a non-seasonally adjusted basis, the number of claims was down to 657 thousand, compared with 757 thousand in the previous week. Also, about 242 thousand people applied for help from the Pandemic Unemployment Assistance scheme, which covers workers that do not qualify for initial claims, compared with 284 thousand in the previous period.</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2021-03-25</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>US Initial Jobless Claims Set to Fall to 730K</b>
                    <div class="comment more">Initial jobless claims are seen dropping to 730 thousand in the week ended March 20th from last week's one-month high of 770 thousand, but remaining well above pre-pandemic levels. Still, claims are expected to fall in the coming weeks following the approval of President Biden's relief package in early March and as several states ease coronavirus-induced restrictions amid the rapid pace of vaccinations. Continuing claims are seen declining to 4.04 million in the March 13th week from 4.12 million the week before, remaining well above February 2020 levels.</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2021-03-25</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>US Weekly Jobless Claims Rise to 1-Month High</b>
                    <div class="comment more">The number of Americans filing for unemployment benefits rose to 770 thousand in the week ended March 13th, the highest level in a month and above market expectations of 700 thousand, as the Covid-19 crisis continued to affect the labor market. Still, claims are seen falling in the coming weeks following the approval of President Biden's relief package and as several states ease coronavirus-induced restrictions. On a non-seasonally adjusted basis, the number of claims was up to 746 thousand, compared with 722 thousand in the previous week. Also, about 282 thousand people applied for help from the Pandemic Unemployment Assistance scheme, which covers workers that do not qualify for initial claims, compared with 479 thousand in the previous period.</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2021-03-18</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                    <div class="list-group-item">
                    <b>US Initial Jobless Claims Set to Fall to 1-Year Low</b>
                    <div class="comment more">Initial jobless claims are seen falling to 700 thousand in the week ended March 13th, their lowest since the pandemic hit the labor market in March 2020, adding to signs of a gradual job recovery. Still, claims continued to be at high levels as the US economic sectors most adversely affected by the COVID-19 crisis remained weak. Continuing claims are seen dropping to 4.07 million in the March 6th week from 4.14 million the week before, remaining well above February 2020 levels.</div>
                   <small style="color: #808080">2021-03-18</small><br/>
                    </div>
                
                 
            
